registry(fILEs);
define
    kantGrammar File='incant/kantGrammar';
    ;
include(kantGrammar);
search reset stack KantGrammar Grokking;
search Utilities UnitTests;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define
    testOP  immediateAction;
    ;
registry(ChainCmpT);
define
    chReq owner source result rule;
    it1;
    qa=2;
    qb=10;
    qc=3;
    qr="untouched";
    chNew code={
        chSrc := argument.CodE;
        chReq.owner  := argument;
        chReq.source := chSrc;
        chReq.rule   := KantGrammar["STARt"];
        if compileIn(chReq);
            chBlock = chReq.result;
            *chBlock :. noPrinT;
            argument :. isActioN;
            argument +< *chBlock;
        };
    chRP code={ print "[" 7 #5d "]":; };
    chA code={ qr = qa < qb < qc; print "CH in" qr:; };
    chK code={ qr = qa < qb && qb < qc; print "CH in" qr:; };
    ;
search ChainCmpT Utilities UnitTests list;
stop();

chainCmpT -- SEQ 570 (Tony, 2026-11-09): a chained comparison (a < b < c) refuses by name on the NEW road (kantGrammar's
EXPRESSIOn), as the old road already does (SEQ 318 R1, exprPinT E2). Rows prefixed CC-. INTERPRETED ONLY: EXPRESSIOn does
not run compiled yet (F-151), so no row here has a compiled arm.

Not run alone: pop.sh builds each row, everything above this stop(); then the row, then print "NEXT" and a sentinel, one
process per row under an alarm. Each row reads exit and sentinel (the next statement runs); the printed value; and the
count of the chain-refusal line by name -- the old road's wording, "REFUSED <first comparison> -- a chained comparison
(a < b < c) -- refused by name (SEQ 318 R1)". The old road's refuse() appends " [line N]"; the new road's cerr does not.

qa 2, qb 10, qc 3, so a first comparison yields 0 or 1 and any "< qc" after it reads 1 whatever the operator: today every
chain row reads 1, silently. A refused statement does nothing, so qr keeps "untouched".

The operator rows put each compareTier operator FIRST (the refusal names the first comparison): the six of R4 plus ~= and
IN, which also carry compareTier. CC-A is the second reach, an action body built through kantGrammar by compileIn with
STARt (newLocalT's shape); its chRP line proves that road ([ 7 ] is the new road's binding). CC-K1, CC-K2 and CC-KA are controls
that must not move: one comparison, and two joined by &&, at the top level and in an action. The old road's control is
exprPinT E2, pinned in its own block.

ROWS, separated by ^ -- id ^ refusal name, empty for a control ^ statement ^ value today ^ value intended (each value as pop.sh reads it, lines joined by |, before NEXT)
